<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The story of SunTour’s slant parallelogram, Shimano SIS and the Japanese gear war that turned indexed shifting into a complete system."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/suntour-shimano-gearkrigen/suntour-honor.jpg"/><title>The slanting line that changed shifting · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/suntour-shimano-gearkrigen/"/><main><article class="quick-page">
<header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">SunTour · Shimano · 1964–1990</p><h1>The slanting line<br/><em>that changed shifting</em></h1><p>A small change to derailleur geometry gave SunTour a lead. Shimano answered with an entire system, not merely a new derailleur.</p></header>
<figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/suntour-shimano-gearkrigen/suntour-honor.jpg`} alt="SunTour Honor rear derailleur fitted to a red bicycle"/><figcaption>A SunTour Honor rear derailleur. Its slanting movement allowed the upper pulley to follow the shape of the freewheel more closely. Photograph: Uberprutser · CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="quick-opening"><p class="section-label">Japan, 1964</p><p>SunTour tilted the parallelogram slightly. It sounds modest. For its competitors, it became a headache lasting about twenty years.</p></section>
<section class="quick-story"><aside><strong>A few degrees</strong><span>were enough to change<br/>the derailleur’s movement</span></aside><div><p>It is tempting to begin at a Shimano drawing board with an engineer cursing a rival’s patent. We do not know that such a moment occurred. But the frustration is easy to imagine: Tetsuo Maeda’s patent was filed in 1964, and the design became associated with SunTour.</p><p>The parallelogram derailleur was not new. The angle was. Instead of moving the chain only sideways, the derailleur also travelled along the sloping profile of the freewheel. Its upper pulley therefore maintained a more consistent distance from both small and large sprockets.</p><blockquote>SunTour did not invent the derailleur. It found the movement the derailleur had been missing.</blockquote></div></section>
<section class="quick-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="quick-invention"><div><p class="section-label">The 1970s advantage</p><h2>The small manufacturer found the large market</h2></div><div><p>On recreational bicycles and lighter racing machines, smooth, accurate shifting mattered more than the prestige attached to the most expensive European parts. SunTour was strong here. Its derailleurs were simple, effective and available at many prices.</p><p>The story is often told as the small inventor’s victory over two giants: Shimano in Japan and Campagnolo in Italy. That is a simplification, but it captures something important. For a time, the best idea was not necessarily the most expensive component.</p></div></section>
<figure class="quick-hub"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/suntour-shimano-gearkrigen/suntour-sprint.jpg`} alt="Loose SunTour Sprint rear derailleur seen from the side" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>SunTour Sprint, photographed and dated to 1986 or 1987 by the photographer. The parallelogram pivots determine the path followed by the pulley. Photograph: otherself · CC BY-SA 2.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="quick-mechanism"><p class="section-label">The mechanics behind the difference</p><div class="mechanism-steps"><div><span>01</span><h3>Friction</h3><p>The rider moves the lever and feels or listens until the chain runs quietly on the sprocket.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h3>Slant path</h3><p>SunTour’s geometry moves the pulley sideways and up or down, allowing it to follow the freewheel more closely.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h3>Indexing</h3><p>Shimano SIS gives the lever fixed clicks. Cable pull, derailleur and sprocket spacing must now work as one system.</p></div></div><p class="safety-note">Slant geometry improved shifting, but did not create indexed gears by itself. A stable indexed system also required precise levers, cables, chain and sprockets.</p></section>
<section class="quick-consequence"><div><p class="section-label">1984: the answer arrives</p><h2>Shimano no longer sold a part. It sold coherence.</h2></div><div><p>When Shimano launched Dura-Ace 7400 with the Shimano Index System in 1984, the gear lever gained fixed positions. Riders no longer had to trim the chain by hand. One click was meant to equal one sprocket.</p><p>The system concept was decisive. Lever, cable pull, derailleur, sprockets and chain had to be matched. Cyclists lost some freedom to mix brands, but shifting became easier to learn and repeat.</p><p>SunTour responded with AccuShift, among other products, but did not gain the same hold on the integrated market. It would be too simple to blame the company’s decline on one patent or product. Currency, production, timing and distribution also mattered. But the move to systems changed the rules.</p></div></section>
<figure class="quick-hub"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/suntour-shimano-gearkrigen/dura-ace.jpg`} alt="Close-up of a Shimano Dura-Ace rear derailleur" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Dura-Ace became the platform for Shimano SIS in 1984. In 1990 Dura-Ace STI combined shifting and braking in the same lever. Photograph: Friedjof · CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="quick-consequence"><div><p class="section-label">1990: hands remain on the bars</p><h2>The final major lever</h2></div><div><p>With the Dura-Ace Dual Control lever in 1990, Shimano placed braking and shifting at the rider’s hands. A rider could change gear during an acceleration without reaching for a down-tube lever.</p><p>The popular version calls this Shimano’s twenty-year revenge. Sources document the chronology, not the motive. But the story has an accurate core: SunTour showed what a small geometric idea could achieve. Shimano showed its strength when the whole bicycle became a system.</p></div></section>
<section class="quick-invention"><div><p class="section-label">The misunderstanding</p><h2>Shimano took the market. Not the company.</h2></div><div><p>It is still said that Shimano eventually bought its old rival. It did not. Shimano took over SunTour’s market position, but the name and remains of the company followed another, messier route.</p><p>SunTour lost orders as bicycle makers selected Shimano’s complete systems. AccuShift never reversed the trend. Yet one poor derailleur was not the sole cause: exchange rates, production problems, late deliveries and weakened confidence all pointed the same way.</p><p>The dates are often confused. SR SunTour’s history says Sakae Ringyo established SR SunTour in 1987 and opened production in Taiwan in 1988. A technical history instead describes Mori Industries buying Sakae in late 1989, taking over the indebted Maeda SunTour around mid-1990, then combining operations under the SR SunTour name.</p><p>The short version stands: Shimano never bought SunTour. The longer version is that there was no single 1988 takeover, but an uneven transition involving Maeda SunTour, Sakae Ringyo, Mori Industries and new Taiwanese production.</p><p>SR SunTour continued. From 1995 it began making suspension forks for mountain bikes, which later became a more visible part of the name than the racing derailleurs that had made SunTour famous.</p></div></section>
<section class="quick-closing"><blockquote>First the derailleur learnt<br/>to follow the freewheel.<br/>Then it learnt <em>to click.</em></blockquote></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and notes</p><div><p>The patent date comes from Tetsuo Maeda’s US patent, filed in 1964. SIS and STI dates follow Shimano’s product history. The long-term “revenge” is a narrative device, not a documented quotation or motive. Sources differ on the transition to SR SunTour, so both the company timeline and technical history are included.</p><ul><li><a href="https://patents.google.com/patent/US3364762A/en">US Patent 3,364,762</a></li><li><a href="https://www.shimano.com/en/100th/history/products/14.html">Shimano: Dura-Ace, SIS and STI</a></li><li><a href="https://www.srsuntour.com/corporate/history/">SR SunTour: company timeline</a></li><li><a href="https://www.italian.sakura.ne.jp/sons_of_biscuits/wp-content/uploads/Sunset_for_Suntour_by_Frank_J_Berto_and_Addendum_by_Paul_Brodek.pdf">International Cycling History Conference: Sunset for SunTour</a></li></ul></div></section>
